      {
        "n": 7,
        "slug": "team-fuel-fundraiser",
        "name": "Team Fuel Fundraiser",
        "oneLiner": "A fundraising storefront where hockey teams, schools, soccer clubs, cadets and community groups each get their own J&W page, share link and live progress bar, and families buy freezer packs and Meal Butler meals with the group's share shown under every price.",
        "headline": "It turns the families around a team or school into J&W customers for about $33 each, paid to the group instead of to Meta, and every order is pre-sold and delivered in one drop on a route J&W already drives.",
        "focus": "Community fundraising that brings new households to J&W",
        "mechanism": "Each school, team, club or community group gets its own J&W team page with a share link, per-player links and a live progress bar. Families buy a short menu at regular store prices: a Team Freezer Pack ($167.50: Premium Chicken Burgers 25-pack $35.50 + 4 kg portioned chicken breast case $92 + Ground Beef Bulk Mix 5 lb $40), a Meal Butler Game-Week Meal Pack ($122.40: 10 cooked 4 oz pre-set meals + 2 Egg White Bites), the Kids Favourite Combo ($95) and the Long Weekend Box ($199). J&W pays the group 20% on Meal Butler and 12% on butcher items from its own margin, shown under every price ($20.10 on the Freezer Pack, $24.48 on the Game-Week Pack), plus 5% on team-link orders for six months after the drop. Orders close on a Sunday and arrive in one labelled drop at the arena or hall on J&W's existing route day (free, no minimum), or families pick up free at 2201 Brimley Rd or take home delivery at the usual fee. Every buyer is asked for CASL consent and offered $10 off a first regular order, and groups get a free 'Fuel for Game Day' talk at $1,500 in sales and a raffle box at $3,000.",
        "modelledOn": {
          "name": "Halenda's Fundraising",
          "url": "https://halendasfundraising.com/",
          "scope": "GTA (Oshawa, Durham Region)",
          "short": "Durham family butcher's meat-stick fundraiser for schools and teams",
          "what": "Halenda's Meats (family-owned since 1979; five Durham stores plus St Jacobs Market; plants in Oshawa and Etobicoke) runs a school and team fundraising program on one product, Pepperoni Stix. Groups buy a case of 240 sticks (24 bags of 10) for $261-$300 and sell each bag at $25, keeping 50-56%. Shipping is $18 a case anywhere in Ontario, pick-up is free. Two modes: 'order the sticks, then sell' (about 95% of groups) and 'collect orders, then buy' with a personalised flyer and order form (about 5%). The site has a two-way profit calculator, a tier table, a sales-rep field on the order form, a school-compliance FAQ and segment success stories.",
          "proof": "First case shipped June 27, 2013; 6 million+ meat sticks sold and 'more than six million dollars' raised for groups (post of Nov 6, 2025). Implied about 450K sticks, 1,880 cases and $0.5M of wholesale revenue a year (ESTIMATE, competitor-07.md). Recent stories: a Newcastle dance studio raised $10,300 (Sept 2026); a 12-year-old BJJ athlete sold 8 cases in a month and raised $1,536 (Aug 2026). Fundraising pages updated Sept 23, 2026. Group count and company revenue not found.",
          "jwEdge": "Halenda's sells cases to the group, which resells bags for cash, so supporters never become Halenda's customers. J&W sells to each family directly on a team page, with consent, so every buyer becomes a J&W customer. Baskets are bigger and are food families already buy: a typical $135 J&W order sends about $21 to the group against $12.50-$14.13 per $25 bag, with no upfront case purchase. J&W adds pork-free packs (the Halenda's stick is pork), cooked Meal Butler meals for sports weeks, its own weekly routes including the Tuesday Durham route past every Halenda's store, and one storefront with current prices instead of three domains with conflicting price tables. Halenda's stays ahead on track record, field reps, awards and a shelf-stable hand-sell product, and groups keep 50-56% there versus 12-20% at J&W."
        },
        "year1": {
          "low": 46000,
          "mid": 129000,
          "high": 271000
        },
        "runRate": 308000,
        "marginPct": 20,
        "startMonth": 2,
        "mathYear1": "Campaigns by drop month: Dec 4, Jan 3, Feb 4, Mar 5, Apr 6, May 6, Jun 3, Jul 1, Aug 1, Sep 4, Oct 5 = 42 campaigns (about 38 groups) x 25 orders x $135 = $141,750 x 0.80 incremental = $113,400. Follow-on: 1,050 orders x 0.9 unique x 70% new = 662 new households x 12% repeat = 79 x 4 orders a year x $130 x 5.26/12 months left = $18,100 x 0.85 = $15,385. Year 1 mid ~$129K. Low: 25 campaigns x 18 orders x $125 x 0.75 = $42,188 + follow-on $3,803 = ~$46K. High: 60 campaigns x 32 orders x $140 x 0.85 = $228,480 + follow-on $42,638 = ~$271K.",
        "mathRunRate": "Year 2: ~85 campaigns (27 returning groups x 1.5 + 45 new) x 27 orders x $138 = $316,710 x 0.80 = $253,368. Follow-on: year-1 cohort 79 households x 60% still active x 4 x $130 = $24,767, plus year-2 cohort 1,343 new households x 12% = 161 x 4 x $130 x 5.67/12 = $39,589; together x 0.85 = $54,702. Run-rate ~$308K. Gross profit after the group share and displacement ~$62K (20%).",
        "mathNotes": [
          "Revenue is counted at full store price. The group share is a cost inside the margin, not a discount: 55% raw at 30% margin + 45% Meal Butler at 45% = 36.75% blended; average group share 55% x 12% + 45% x 20% = 15.6% (about $21 on a $135 order); margin after the share 21.15%. Follow-on orders earn 36.75% less 5% on the ~60% placed through the team link = 33.75%.",
          "Displacement is charged too: the 20% of fundraiser sales existing customers would have placed anyway now carries the group share ($28,350 x 15.6% = $4,423 in year 1; $9,881 in year 2). Gross profit after share and displacement: year 1 $24,754 (19% of counted revenue), year 2 $62,168 (20%), hence marginPct 20.",
          "Contribution after J&W's running costs: year 1 $24,754 - $22,066 running - $3,200 setup = about -$0.5K (break-even); year 2 $62,168 - $40,000 = about +$22K. The real return is about 2,000 new households in two years (662 + 1,343).",
          "Group share per new household: $22,113 / 662 = about $33 in year 1 and $37 in year 2, against $60-120 paid-ads CAC (model-assumptions.md).",
          "Monthly shape, year 1 gross booked: Nov $0, Dec $13.5K, Jan $10.5K, Feb $14.1K, Mar $17.8K, Apr $21.6K, May $22.1K, Jun $12.4K, Jul $5.9K, Aug $6.0K, Sep $16.2K, Oct $19.9K = $159.9K gross, $128.8K after incrementality.",
          "Sanity: year 1 is about 5% of J&W's ~$2.5M mid-case revenue, run-rate about 12%. Halenda's implied ~1,880 cases a year at an assumed 6 cases per group is roughly 300 campaigns a year (group count not found); J&W's 85 in year 2 is about 28% of that, across a wider route area. The GTHL alone has 493 teams.",
          "Trimmed from the selection's year-2 idea (100 groups x $4,000 plus a $0.16-0.31M DTC tail), as competitor-07.md advised: only two buyer cohorts exist by year 2, so the follow-on tail is about $55K.",
          "Not counted: Team Fuel Jerky (in development), and any later Butcher Club (Play 3), Goal Plan (Play 1), AI Coach (Play 6), gifting (Play 5), At Work (Play 4) or wholesale (Play 2) revenue from fundraiser buyers or clubs. Follow-on here is one-time regular orders only; the 0.85 factor partly covers overlap."
        ],
