            "url": "https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/hellofresh-is-investing-70-million-to-have-ai-help-plan-dinner"
          },
          {
            "company": "JBS USA (Völur)",
            "useCase": "AI carcass cutting plans",
            "result": "Daily planning from 2–6 hours to 15–30 seconds (vendor figure)",
            "url": "https://www.volur.ai/solutions"
          },
          {
            "company": "Qrew (Clay)",
            "useCase": "AI B2B prospecting",
            "result": "About twice the positive replies and 40% more meetings with half the sales team (vendor case study)",
            "url": "https://clay.com/blog/qrew-case-study"
          }
        ]
      },
      "proposal": {
        "title": "The J&W growth plan",
        "subtitle": "Five plays in year one, sixteen AI agents on the desk work, and a path to about $1M a year in new sales from 2028. Prepared by Minastech for Bill Fehr and Stavros Trougakos, October 16, 2026.",
        "summary": [
          "J&W already has the hard parts of a protein brand. Its own Ontario-inspected plant. More than 30 years of butchery. About 292 cooked portions with no added salt, sugar or oil, frozen packs that keep for months, and weekly routes across Ontario. What it lacks is the packaging around those assets: standing orders, business buyers, partner channels and a website that shows what 611 Google reviewers already say.",
          "We studied about 60 businesses and built seven clickable prototypes, each modelled on one that already makes the idea pay, from Power Kitchen 10 km away to Omaha Steaks in Nebraska. Each runs on J&W's real products, prices and photos. Each comes with costs, KPIs and the AI agents that would do the desk work. A separate CFO pass checked every number.",
          "For year one we recommend five of the seven: Corporate Gift Studio, Goal Plan Builder, Butcher Club, J&W at Work and the AI Coach. After 28% off for overlap and for one small team running five launches, they add about $0.80M in year 1 (November 2026 to October 2027) and about $1.94M in year 2. That is +32% and +78% on an estimated $2.5M today, and about $302K and $742K of gross profit. Year 1 is a build year: its gross profit roughly pays for the plays' own running costs. Year 2 is where the return shows.",
          "The narrower $1M path, Gifts now, Plans in December, Offices in January, reaches about $1.0M a year in annualised sales at the end of 2027 and averages about $1.25M in year 2, with about $494K of gross profit. Its first-year sales are only about $0.45M, and in the low case it reaches about $0.62M. Two conditions come with it. Plant and packing capacity must be confirmed before the January push. And J&W needs a growth or operations coordinator, so Lucas and Tasia are not carrying four launches at once."
        ],
        "recommendation": "Start with three, in this order. First, the Corporate Gift Studio, this month: it is the only cash available in Q4 2026, it costs about $5.5K to start, and it opens the door to HR teams and office managers. Second, the Goal Plan Builder, live in December and pushed in January: it is the highest-scoring idea, it earns about 40% on meals J&W already cooks, and the AI Coach is built on it. Third, J&W at Work, piloting with three workplaces in January when wellness budgets reset, sold to the employers met through gifting. Those three are the $1M path. Two more run alongside because timing and kitchen load favour them. Butcher Club opens as a Wild Fork switch campaign in November, because those stores close December 14 and the club loads the butchery, not the kitchen. The AI Coach adds the Personal Macro Plan tier in January, capped at about 100 clients until the weigh-and-pack run is proven. Pro Partner Portal and Team Fuel stay ready as backups: Pro Partner if At Work stalls by June 2027, Team Fuel for the fall 2027 hockey season.",
        "today": [
          "J&W is a family business of about 10 to 18 people, with estimated sales of about $2.5M a year (range $1.2M to $4.0M; no figure is published). It sells through the Brimley Rd store, jwfoods.com, six weekly route runs and some informal wholesale. Customers rate it highly, with 4.8 stars from 611 Google reviews, about twice the count of early 2023, and trainers send their clients. Prices are well below premium rivals: AAA ribeye at about $23 to $25/lb against $31 to $60/lb at Toronto's premium butchers, and pre-set meals at a median $12.99 against $14 to $30 at premium GTA meal-prep brands.",
          "The online business does not show any of that yet. There is no subscription, so regulars rebuild the same cart every week. The reviews never reach the product pages. There is no page for gyms, employers or gift buyers, no email platform and no Google Analytics. Half the catalogue has no description, and J&W barely appears in searches for meal prep or butchers in Toronto. Meanwhile the market has opened up. Wild Fork is leaving Ontario, and its Whitby, Oakville, Ancaster and Newmarket stores, all on J&W routes, close by December 14. LiveFit has closed, Goodfood is in creditor protection, and GLP-1 users want exactly the small, protein-dense portions J&W already cooks."
        ],
        "competitorsIntro": [
          "Each prototype is modelled on one business that already makes the idea pay. Four are in the GTA or Canada: Power Kitchen, truLOCAL, WeCook and Halenda's. Three are abroad, used only where no local player runs the model at real scale: Icon Meals, Omaha Steaks and Calo.",
          "We checked every one on October 16, 2026, from their own sites, catalogues and filings. We borrowed mechanics, not words or designs, and we say where each one is weak. In every case J&W has something they do not: its own inspected plant, butcher prices, a clean recipe promise and its own routes."
        ],
        "playsIntro": [
          "Each play is a clickable prototype built on J&W's real products, prices and photos. New items are labelled Concept. Every page has a customer view and a team view that shows what the AI agents have drafted and what is waiting for a person to approve.",
          "The numbers on each page are year-1 and year-2 estimates of new sales, after removing orders J&W would have had anyway, and they were checked by a separate CFO pass. Five plays are in the year-one plan. Pro Partner and Team Fuel are built and ready as backups."
        ],
        "howWeWork": [
          "Shopify first. We build on the store J&W already runs, with tools it keeps if we ever part ways: Shopify subscriptions and B2B catalogues, Klaviyo for email and SMS, a reviews app and Google Analytics. The custom pieces, such as the quiz, the gift studio and the coach, sit inside the theme and read the same catalogue. There is no new platform for the team to learn and nothing that locks J&W in.",
          "Agents draft, people approve. Sixteen AI agents take on the desk work behind every order: answering, quoting, confirming, planning, labelling, routing and invoicing. Ten are built in the first 90 days and six follow in months 4 to 9. A named person at J&W approves anything that reaches a customer, changes a price, moves money or commits the plant. Allergy and medical questions always go to a person. Cooking, cutting, packing and driving stay with the team.",
          "Weekly reporting. Every Monday you get one page: sales by play against the plan, subscribers and churn, accounts and gifts, cost per new customer, kitchen load and route stops, and the three decisions we need from you that week. We meet monthly. At day 90 we review every play against its pass mark and agree to keep it, fix it or stop it."
        ],
        "ninety": [
          {
            "when": "Week 1 (Oct 19–23)",
            "what": "Kick-off at Brimley Rd. Access to Shopify, Smile.io, Meta and the Google Business Profile. Install Google Analytics and Klaviyo. Name one approver per area. Fix the Taste of Greece box description.",
            "who": "Minastech with Bill, Stavros and Lucas"
          },
          {
            "when": "Weeks 1–6 (Oct 19–Nov 30)",
            "what": "Gift Studio call list (first test 1): 40 past business buyers, realtors and HR contacts get the four gift levels, a logo card and a tasting box.",
            "who": "Lucas as Gift Desk lead; Minastech drafts the outreach"
          },
          {
            "when": "Weeks 2–3 (Oct 26–Nov 6)",
            "what": "Reviews app live and seeded from recent buyers. Butler Concierge chat on the site. Hours, postal code, delivery minimum and the 'CFIA Approved' wording made consistent everywhere.",
            "who": "Tasia approves; Minastech builds"
          },
          {
            "when": "Week 3 (from Nov 2)",
            "what": "Wild Fork switch campaign (first test 4): a welcome page and geo ads in Whitby, Oakville, Ancaster and Newmarket. The Butcher Club founding-member list opens.",
            "who": "Minastech runs it; head butcher sets the box menu; owners set the ad cap"
          },
          {
            "when": "Week 4 (Nov 9)",
            "what": "Corporate Gift Studio goes live with recipient upload, logo cards, Pick-a-Box certificates and route-day sorting. Business Desk and Account Scout agents start drafting quotes and outreach.",
            "who": "Minastech; Lucas approves quotes, owners approve any over $5,000"
          },
          {
            "when": "Weeks 4–6 (November)",
            "what": "Plant capacity and licence check (first test 2): a two-day time study on the cooking and packing lines, and questions to J&W's OMAFA inspector on partner labels, gifts and the licence's limits.",
            "who": "Bill, Stavros and the kitchen lead; Minastech prepares the study sheet"
          },
          {
            "when": "Weeks 5–7 (Nov 16–Dec 4)",
            "what": "Macros for about 31 plan items computed by the Nutrition & Safety agent and signed off by a partner dietitian. Quiz, six plans and Subscribe & Save built and tested. Butcher Club boxes and the Production Planner set up.",
            "who": "Minastech, the partner dietitian, Lucas and the kitchen lead"
          },
          {
            "when": "Weeks 7–9 (Dec 1–18)",
            "what": "Goal Plan pre-sale to existing buyers (first test 3) with a $25 switch credit. First Butcher Club boxes on route days. Holiday gift peak, with Monday prep sheets from the Production Planner.",
            "who": "Lucas, the kitchen lead and drivers; Minastech watches the numbers daily"
          },
          {
            "when": "Weeks 11–12 (Jan 4–15)",
            "what": "January push: Goal Plans open to everyone, the AI Coach and Personal Macro Plans launch (capped at about 100 clients), and the J&W at Work pilot starts at three workplaces (first test 5).",
            "who": "Lucas and the new coordinator; Minastech"
          },
          {
            "when": "Day 90 (week of Jan 18)",
            "what": "KPI review: keep, fix or stop each play against its pass mark. Confirm the second shift and the coordinator hire. Agree the next 90 days and the add-on agents.",
            "who": "Minastech with Bill and Stavros"
          }
        ],
        "kpis": [
          "Gift Studio: 10+ companies and 200+ gifts booked by November 30 at an average of $110 or more, and about 18 corporate accounts by the end of the holiday season.",
          "Goal Plans: 60+ subscribers within 30 days of the December pre-sale, 85% or more taking a second delivery, and skips under 20%.",
          "Butcher Club: 50+ founding members or first orders from the Wild Fork towns, at a paid cost per new customer under $90.",
          "Capacity: room for about 2,000 extra cooked items a week confirmed on current shifts, or a costed second shift approved.",
          "J&W at Work: three pilot workplaces live, with at least one employer ready to agree a meal subsidy.",
          "Reviews: at least five on-site reviews on each of the top 30 sellers, and a reply to every 1- to 3-star review within two business days.",
          "Agents: the 10 core agents live, with every customer message, price change and plant commitment approved by a named person, and every medical or allergy mention reaching a person within one business day.",
          "Data: Google Analytics, Klaviyo and the Monday report running, and J&W's real revenue split in place of our $2.5M estimate."
        ],
        "risks": [
          {
            "risk": "Provincial licence limits. Meat from an Ontario-inspected plant can only be sold inside Ontario, and partner freezers, gifts and new products depend on label and process rules.",
            "mitigation": "Every play is designed for Ontario and J&W's existing routes. We ask J&W's OMAFA inspector what the licence covers in November, reconcile the 'CFIA Approved' claim, and the gift studio declines out-of-province addresses automatically."
          },
          {
            "risk": "Plant capacity. By year 2 the five-play plan adds about 4,100 cooked items and about 300 delivery stops a week, against an estimated 60 to 160 delivery orders a week today.",
            "mitigation": "A two-day time study before the January push, the AI Coach capped at about 100 clients, and launches spaced out. A supervised second shift, cold storage and probably a second reefer van (lease about $1.8K to $2.6K a month) are budgeted at $60K to $100K a year."
          },
          {
            "risk": "Cold chain. More stops, office freezers and a December peak raise the chance of thawed boxes, the top complaint at truLOCAL and Icon.",
            "mitigation": "J&W's own routes instead of couriers, insulated totes, Wi-Fi temperature sensors and logs on every loaned freezer, delivery-window texts, and a KPI of under 1% thaw or quality complaints."
          },
          {
            "risk": "Subscription churn. This is J&W's first subscription, and 44% of box cancellations happen in the first 90 days.",
            "mitigation": "Modelled at 10% monthly churn, not the best case. Skip, pause and Freezer Fill are offered before cancel, failed payments are followed up, save offers stay inside owner limits, and one-off ordering remains for people who do not want a subscription."
          },
          {
            "risk": "Key-person dependence. The brand rests on a few people, and Lucas or Tasia are named lead on several plays, roughly 35 to 65 hours a week combined at peak.",
            "mitigation": "Hire a growth or operations coordinator before January. Agents take the desk work, approval rules are written down, and the Monday brief keeps the owners in charge without daily firefighting."
          },
          {
            "risk": "Data and privacy (PIPEDA and CASL). Coach chats can include health details, employers share staff lists, and fundraisers involve families with children.",
            "mitigation": "Collect only what each play needs, with clear consent screens. SMS goes only to people with express consent and an easy Reply STOP. Health details are never used for ads, records are deleted on request, the privacy policy is updated, and each tool's data terms are reviewed before launch."
          },
          {
            "risk": "AI errors. An agent could quote a wrong price, macro or allergen answer.",
            "mitigation": "Agents read only the approved catalogue, prices and routes. A named person approves anything that reaches a customer, changes a price or commits the plant. Allergen and medical questions always go to a person, a dietitian signs off macros, and we spot-check replies weekly."
          },
          {
            "risk": "B2B credit. Employers and gift buyers will ask for invoices and terms, with most gift volume landing in December.",
            "mitigation": "Card or prepayment on first orders, net-15 only after approval, a credit limit per account, a Receivables Desk agent chasing invoices from month 4, and a reserve of about 0.5% of B2B sales."
          },
          {
            "risk": "Labels and claims. The front-of-package nutrition symbol is mandatory since January 2026, and claims like 'high protein' or 'GLP-1' are regulated.",
            "mitigation": "Macros are marked as estimates until lab tests are done, labels are checked against Health Canada and CFIA guidance, and a registered dietitian approves any health wording."
          },
          {
            "risk": "Demand falls short. In the low case the $1M path reaches only about $0.62M.",
            "mitigation": "Every first test has a pass mark, and the day-90 review keeps, fixes or stops each play. Two backups are named: Pro Partner if At Work stalls by June 2027, and a clinic referral program if Goal Plans run behind."
          }
        ],
        "asks": [
          "Admin access to Shopify, Smile.io, Meta Business and the Google Business Profile, plus an export of the last 24 months of Shopify orders.",
          "Your real revenue split (store, online, wholesale), online order value, active customers and repeat rate, so we can replace our $2.5M estimate in month 1.",
          "Two days of kitchen and packing time for the capacity study, and today's route stops per day and van count.",
          "Your OMAFA inspector's contact and licence details, and clarity on the 'CFIA Approved' wording and the chicken quota.",
          "Recipes and cooked yields for about 31 plan items, and approval to engage a partner registered dietitian.",
          "One named approver per area (for example Lucas for plans and campaigns, Tasia for reviews and Instagram, the kitchen lead for production, Bill or Stavros for prices and quotes over $5,000), and about one hour a week from the owners.",
          "A list of past business buyers, realtors and HR contacts for the Gift Studio call list, and 20 tasting boxes at cost.",
          "A decision on hiring a growth or operations coordinator before the January push."
        ]
      }
    }
  },
